::CW_U05_L05_Q01::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A writer has a revised poem, peer feedback notes, and a class online publication space. What is the best next step before publication?{
~A. Ignore the feedback notes.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered before publication. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Post the oldest draft.#Incorrect. Publication should use the revised, checked version.
~C. Remove the title automatically.#Incorrect. A title often helps online readers enter the piece.
=D. Check whether the poem's purpose, format, title, and conventions are publication-ready.#Correct. This completes a responsible publication-readiness review.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q02::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which statement best defines online collaborative publication?{
~A. Posting a rough draft without review.#Incorrect. That is posting, not publication-ready collaboration.
=B. Preparing writing for an online audience through revision, format checks, and shared review roles.#Correct. This matches the lesson's publication workflow.
~C. Letting one peer rewrite the whole piece.#Incorrect. Collaboration should not erase the writer's voice.
~D. Choosing a font before considering meaning.#Incorrect. Visual choices are not enough. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q03::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A writer's purpose is to create a quiet mood. Which feedback decision best protects that purpose?{
~A. Accept every suggestion automatically.#Incorrect. Not every suggestion supports the writer's purpose.
~B. Reject all feedback automatically.#Incorrect. Some feedback may improve the piece. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Accept feedback that strengthens quiet imagery and reject feedback that makes the ending loud without a purpose.#Correct. This evaluates feedback against the writer's intended effect.
~D. Change the piece into a comedy because one peer prefers comedy.#Incorrect. This replaces the writer's purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q04::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which collaborative role is responsible for checking whether the piece is appropriate for the intended online class space?{
=A. Final reviewer.#Correct. A final reviewer checks readiness, including appropriateness for the audience and space.
~B. Someone who only counts words.#Incorrect. Word count alone does not determine appropriateness.
~C. Someone who changes the writer's theme.#Incorrect. The role should not replace the writer's purpose.
~D. Random commenter.#Incorrect. Publication roles should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q05::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] What is the main reason to include a clear title in an online publication entry?{
~A. It replaces revision.#Incorrect. A title does not remove the need for revision.
=B. It prepares the reader to enter the piece.#Correct. A title can guide the reader's first understanding.
~C. It proves the piece is perfect.#Incorrect. A title alone does not prove readiness.
~D. It lets the writer skip formatting.#Incorrect. Formatting still matters. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q06::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which publication checklist item should happen after receiving peer feedback?{
~A. Delete the piece immediately.#Incorrect. Feedback should be evaluated, not used as a reason to delete automatically.
~B. Use every suggestion without thinking.#Incorrect. Feedback must be checked against purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Ignore feedback because publication is individual only.#Incorrect. This lesson focuses on collaborative publication. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Decide which feedback improves purpose, clarity, format, or reader effect.#Correct. This turns feedback into purposeful revision. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q07::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which item is least publication-ready?{
~A. A revised story with clear paragraphing and a final conventions check.#Incorrect. This sounds publication-ready. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. A poem with purposeful stanza breaks and a brief author note.#Incorrect. These choices can prepare readers well. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. A rough draft copied into the online space with no title, formatting, or review.#Correct. This skips key publication checks. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. A script with character labels and checked stage directions.#Incorrect. These support script publication quality. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q08::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] What should an author note do?{
=A. Provide helpful context or craft focus without replacing the creative work.#Correct. An author note should prepare the reader while keeping the piece central.
~B. Explain every line so readers do not need the creative piece.#Incorrect. That overexplains and replaces the work. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Include private personal information.#Incorrect. Online publication should protect privacy and appropriateness.
~D. Distract readers from the title.#Incorrect. The author note should support the publication entry.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q09::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A peer suggests changing a script into a poem, but the writer's purpose depends on performance and spoken dialogue. What should the writer do?{
~A. Change it into a poem automatically.#Incorrect. This may damage the writer's purpose.
=B. Consider the feedback but keep the script form if performance is central to the piece.#Correct. The writer evaluates feedback against purpose and format.
~C. Reject the peer as a person.#Incorrect. Feedback decisions should stay respectful and focused on the work.
~D. Remove all dialogue.#Incorrect. Dialogue is central to the writer's purpose.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q10::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which choice best shows a format checker doing the role correctly?{
~A. The checker rewrites the theme without permission.#Incorrect. That goes beyond format checking. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. The checker ignores line breaks in a poem.#Incorrect. Line breaks matter in poem format. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. The checker notes that a new speaker in prose dialogue needs a new paragraph.#Correct. This checks format-specific reader clarity. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. The checker only says "good."#Incorrect. This gives no format information. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q11::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which online publication decision is safest and most appropriate?{
~A. Include personal addresses in an author note.#Incorrect. Personal information should not be shared in a class publication space.
~B. Post another student's work without permission.#Incorrect. Collaborative publication must respect authorship. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Publish comments that mock a peer.#Incorrect. Publication must remain respectful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Use a school-appropriate excerpt and avoid private identifying details.#Correct. This protects audience appropriateness and privacy. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q12::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A piece has strong content but many distracting errors. What should happen before online publication?{
~A. Publish it anyway because content is all that matters.#Incorrect. Conventions affect readability. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Complete a final conventions check while preserving the writer's voice.#Correct. Publication readiness includes clarity and conventions. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Change the genre immediately.#Incorrect. The issue is errors, not necessarily genre.
~D. Remove the title only.#Incorrect. That does not address distracting errors. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q13::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which sentence best explains a collaborative publication decision?{
=A. I accepted the suggestion to clarify the title because it helps readers understand the poem's central image.#Correct. This names a decision and explains reader effect.
~B. I changed it because someone told me to.#Incorrect. This does not show reasoning. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. I ignored all feedback because collaboration is useless.#Incorrect. This rejects the process. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. I changed everything so the piece no longer sounds like mine.#Incorrect. Collaboration should preserve voice and purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q14::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which publication element helps readers know what they are about to read?{
~A. A missing title.#Incorrect. A missing title does not guide readers.
~B. Unchecked spacing.#Incorrect. Unchecked spacing may confuse readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. A clear title and, when useful, a brief author note.#Correct. These elements prepare the reader. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Private personal data.#Incorrect. This is not appropriate. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q15::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] What is the problem with accepting every reviewer suggestion?{
~A. It always improves the piece.#Incorrect. Suggestions may conflict with purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. It makes format checks unnecessary.#Incorrect. Format still must be checked. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. It proves the writer is collaborative.#Incorrect. Collaboration is thoughtful, not automatic. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. It can weaken the writer's purpose, voice, or intended effect.#Correct. Writers need to evaluate feedback thoughtfully. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q16::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which sequence best matches publication workflow?{
~A. Post, revise later, ignore readers, remove title.#Incorrect. This reverses readiness checks. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Identify purpose, consider feedback, check format, prepare the reader, complete final review.#Correct. This follows the lesson sequence. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~C. Choose colors, skip feedback, delete draft, submit nothing.#Incorrect. This does not support publication. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Ask one peer to rewrite everything, then publish without checking.#Incorrect. This weakens authorship and readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q17::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which comment best supports a writer preparing for publication?{
=A. The final stanza break creates a useful pause; check whether the second stanza also needs that same clarity.#Correct. This gives specific, format-focused publication feedback.
~B. Your line breaks are random.#Incorrect. This identifies a problem but does not yet guide revision.
~C. I like it.#Incorrect. This is too general. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Do whatever I would do.#Incorrect. This centers the reviewer instead of the writer.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q18::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] What should a writer do if a piece is not appropriate for the intended online audience?{
~A. Publish it anyway.#Incorrect. Audience appropriateness is required. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Hide the issue with a vague title.#Incorrect. A title does not solve an appropriateness issue.
=C. Revise, select a different excerpt, or ask the TOR for guidance before submitting it.#Correct. This handles appropriateness responsibly. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Blame the audience.#Incorrect. Writers must consider the publication space. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q19::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which statement best describes collaboration in this lesson?{
~A. Collaboration means the loudest reviewer controls the piece.#Incorrect. That does not protect author purpose. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Collaboration means no one revises.#Incorrect. Revision is part of publication readiness. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Collaboration means format does not matter.#Incorrect. Format matters for online readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=D. Collaboration means writers and reviewers use roles to improve readiness while respecting the writer's purpose.#Correct. This captures the purpose of collaborative publication.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q20::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Why should formatting be checked before online publication?{
~A. Formatting is only decoration.#Incorrect. Format can carry meaning and clarity. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=B. Formatting can affect readability, genre expectations, pacing, and reader understanding.#Correct. Format directly shapes the reader's experience.
~C. Formatting replaces purpose.#Incorrect. Format should support purpose, not replace it.
~D. Formatting should always be random.#Incorrect. Format choices should be purposeful. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q21::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which title decision is strongest?{
~A. Use no title because titles never matter.#Incorrect. Titles often guide online readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Use a misleading title unrelated to the piece.#Incorrect. This can confuse readers. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
=C. Choose a title that connects to the central image or purpose of the piece.#Correct. A purposeful title prepares the reader. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
~D. Use private information as the title.#Incorrect. Publication should protect privacy. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q22::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] A peer says, "This is ready to publish," but the piece has no clear ending and no format check. What should the writer do?{
=A. Thank the peer, then complete the ending and format checks before publication.#Correct. This uses feedback while still checking readiness.
~B. Publish immediately because one peer approved it.#Incorrect. One approval does not replace readiness checks.
~C. Ignore the peer rudely.#Incorrect. The writer can respond respectfully. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Remove all formatting.#Incorrect. The problem is missing format review, not too much formatting.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q23::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which final review question is most useful?{
~A. Did I post as quickly as possible?#Incorrect. Speed is not the goal. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~B. Did I ignore the reader?#Incorrect. Publication should consider the reader. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Did I erase the writer's voice?#Incorrect. The writer's voice should be protected.
=D. Is the piece revised, formatted, appropriate, and clear for the intended online audience?#Correct. This covers key publication readiness criteria. This answer matches the lesson skill and is supported by the specific creative-writing evidence in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q24::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which action respects authorship in collaborative publication?{
=A. Giving feedback while leaving final craft decisions to the writer and teacher-guided requirements.#Correct. This supports collaboration and respects the author.
~B. Publishing someone else's draft without permission.#Incorrect. This violates authorship. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~C. Changing the writer's name.#Incorrect. That does not respect authorship. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. Replacing the whole piece with your own.#Incorrect. That erases the author. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
}

::CW_U05_L05_Q25::[MLA.CW.PUB.01] Which mastery response best fits Lesson 5?{
~A. My piece is ready because I typed it once.#Incorrect. A typed draft still needs review and readiness checks.
=B. My piece is ready for online class publication because I revised from feedback, checked format, prepared the reader with a title, and confirmed audience appropriateness.#Correct. This includes the key publication-readiness components.
~C. My piece is ready because I ignored feedback.#Incorrect. Feedback should be considered. This choice is not supported by the provided context or lesson skill; choose the option grounded in the prompt.
~D. My piece is ready because I used random spacing.#Incorrect. Random spacing does not prove publication quality.
}
